{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015dUKfGu2ZWG6i9eYDT7Heu","type":"tool_result","content":"3438: * plus the destination with its leading dots dropped; a marked-section base sits\n3445:function layoutMoves(manifest) {\n5541:// dots dropped, so no copy is a hidden folder.\n5548:// The earlier place for an adopted copy: the same path with the leading dots\n5707:  // An excluded path's copy may still sit at its earlier dot-dropped place, so\n57:| `.build-lock` | Present only while one of the engine's writing runs holds it: an `apply`, a capture, an adopt, a relayout, a live re-pin or a rollback |\n92:| `LAYOUT   <overlay>: <field> <from> belongs at <to>` | The copy the entry names sits off its layout path. A destination under `.skilled/` lays out under `framework/`, so `.skilled/skills/x` belongs at `framework/skills/x`, and `.skilled` itself at `framework`. Any other destination lays out under `root/`, with its leading dots dropped, so `.claude/settings.json` belongs at `root/claude/settings.json`. `<field>` is `source`, `fragment` or `base`, and a `marked-section` entry can print one line for each. The `base` path carries `.base` before the extension. `status` prints at most 20 of these lines, then one overflow line: `LAYOUT   … <n> more; run node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/overlay-engine.cjs apply --relayout` when no hidden move sits under `root/skilled`, `LAYOUT   … <n> more under root/skilled; change the destination or remove the entry` when every hidden move does, and `LAYOUT   … <n> more; run node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/overlay-engine.cjs apply --relayout for <m>, and change the destination or remove the entry for the <b> under root/skilled` when they mix. `status --json` keeps every move in `offLayout`. A LAYOUT line never fails the status | `/doctor:overlay apply --relayout`; when `<to>` sits under `root/skilled` the line ends `change the destination or remove the entry` instead, because relayout refuses that move |\n96:`apply --relayout` moves each off-layout copy onto its layout path, `framework/` for a destination under `.skilled/` and `root/` for any other, and with it a `marked-section` entry's `fragment` and `base`. It copies every path it moves into `<overlayDir>/relayout-runs/<stamp>/` first, inside the overlay folder and keyed from its root, so a sibling folder keeps its own backup, then points the entry's fields and the manifest's layout links at the new path. It refuses rather than overwrite a path that already exists, and then it writes nothing. It refuses a move to a path under `root/skilled/` too, such as one for a destination named `skilled/...` at the tree root, because that folder holds no copy: the framework's copies live under `framework/` alone, so one destination never has two sources. A manifest whose `source`, `fragment` or `base` already sits there is a blocked overlay. The run retargets each live link that still named a moved copy and prints a `relinked` line for it. The plain `apply` that follows writes the destinations from the moved copies. When a move, the manifest write or a relink fails part way, the run puts back every copy it moved, the manifest and every link it retargeted, removes its own `relayout-runs/<stamp>/` folder, prints `error: relayout failed while <step>: <message>; every moved copy, the manifest and the links are back as they were` and exits 2. If that restore fails too, the line names the path it could not restore, keeps the backup, names `relayout-runs/<stamp>` as the place that holds every moved copy and the manifest as it was, and the run exits 2. If everything is back but the backup folder cannot be removed, the line says so, names the backup and the reason, and the run exits 2.\n136:`apply`, a capture, an adopt, a relayout, a live re-pin and a rollback each take `<overlayDir>/.build-lock` and remove it when they finish, also on failure. The owner line records the process id and the host that wrote it, as `pid=<n> host=<name>`. A lock whose owner has ended on this host is leftover state, so the next run takes it over and prints one note naming whose lock it was. A `repin --stage` does not take it: it reads a dead owner's line, prints `note: the overlay lock belongs to a run that has ended:`, and leaves the lock for the next writing run. A line with a process id and no `host=` token came from an engine that only ever ran on the machine it was installed in, so that id is probed here too. The takeover runs behind `<overlayDir>/.build-lock.takeover`, and only one run at a time can create that file. A marker older than a minute is what a run killed mid-takeover leaves behind, so the next run clears it instead of waiting for it to go. Neither leftover is deleted by its shared path. The lock and the marker are each renamed aside first and deleted only when the renamed file is the one that was judged stale, and a file another run wrote in the meantime is put back, unless another file already stands at the path, in which case the set-aside file is dropped. A marker put back this way can outlive its run, and a takeover then refuses for up to a minute and says to run it again. A run killed in that instant can leave a `.claim` file beside the lock, which is safe to delete. A lock with a live owner, a lock another host wrote, or a lock with no process id in its line still refuses, and that refusal prints the owner line and the lock file path. Remove such a file by hand once you know its run has ended.\n188:`/doctor:overlay apply --adopt <path>` turns the path into a manifest entry. It copies the file or folder into the overlay folder at its layout path, under `framework/` for a path under `.skilled/` and under `root/` for any other, with its leading dots dropped, then appends a `file` or `directory` entry and applies. An upstream file is pinned to upstream's bytes at the manifest's pin, read from the checkout `pin.checkout` names, so a later re-pin reports it as `upstream-moved`. A folder's token-named source files go into the entry's `protectedPaths`. Adopt refuses a generated path, a path an overlay already owns, a `.env` file, a token-named file that is not source or prose, a folder that holds upstream files, and a path whose copy would land under `root/skilled/`.\n263:The layout path puts each copy under `framework/` when its destination sits under `.skilled/` and under `root/` otherwise. A tree whose overlay folder still keeps its copies at the older paths, the destination with its leading dots dropped and no prefix, needs no change to keep working:\n267:- The one action is `/doctor:overlay apply --relayout`, run once. It moves every copy `offLayout` lists, backs each one up first, and points the manifest at the new paths. The next `status` prints no `LAYOUT` line.\n268:- Skipping it breaks nothing. Until it runs, though, `apply --adopt` writes each new entry at its `framework/` or `root/` path, so the folder holds both layouts side by side until the relayout moves the older copies.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"faa6b93c-ce84-436d-8875-372fa8efb878","uuid":"ebcac42e-57b8-4f09-b71a-6d376191fcc2","timestamp":"2026-11-20T16:39:23.652Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"3438: * plus the destination with its leading dots dropped; a marked-section base sits\n3445:function layoutMoves(manifest) {\n5541:// dots dropped, so no copy is a hidden folder.\n5548:// The earlier place for an adopted copy: the same path with the leading dots\n5707:  // An excluded path's copy may still sit at its earlier dot-dropped place, so\n57:| `.build-lock` | Present only while one of the engine's writing runs holds it: an `apply`, a capture, an adopt, a relayout, a live re-pin or a rollback |\n92:| `LAYOUT   <overlay>: <field> <from> belongs at <to>` | The copy the entry names sits off its layout path. A destination under `.skilled/` lays out under `framework/`, so `.skilled/skills/x` belongs at `framework/skills/x`, and `.skilled` itself at `framework`. Any other destination lays out under `root/`, with its leading dots dropped, so `.claude/settings.json` belongs at `root/claude/settings.json`. `<field>` is `source`, `fragment` or `base`, and a `marked-section` entry can print one line for each. The `base` path carries `.base` before the extension. `status` prints at most 20 of these lines, then one overflow line: `LAYOUT   … <n> more; run node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/overlay-engine.cjs apply --relayout` when no hidden move sits under `root/skilled`, `LAYOUT   … <n> more under root/skilled; change the destination or remove the entry` when every hidden move does, and `LAYOUT   … <n> more; run node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/overlay-engine.cjs apply --relayout for <m>, and change the destination or remove the entry for the <b> under root/skilled` when they mix. `status --json` keeps every move in `offLayout`. A LAYOUT line never fails the status | `/doctor:overlay apply --relayout`; when `<to>` sits under `root/skilled` the line ends `change the destination or remove the entry` instead, because relayout refuses that move |\n96:`apply --relayout` moves each off-layout copy onto its layout path, `framework/` for a destination under `.skilled/` and `root/` for any other, and with it a `marked-section` entry's `fragment` and `base`. It copies every path it moves into `<overlayDir>/relayout-runs/<stamp>/` first, inside the overlay folder and keyed from its root, so a sibling folder keeps its own backup, then points the entry's fields and the manifest's layout links at the new path. It refuses rather than overwrite a path that already exists, and then it writes nothing. It refuses a move to a path under `root/skilled/` too, such as one for a destination named `skilled/...` at the tree root, because that folder holds no copy: the framework's copies live under `framework/` alone, so one destination never has two sources. A manifest whose `source`, `fragment` or `base` already sits there is a blocked overlay. The run retargets each live link that still named a moved copy and prints a `relinked` line for it. The plain `apply` that follows writes the destinations from the moved copies. When a move, the manifest write or a relink fails part way, the run puts back every copy it moved, the manifest and every link it retargeted, removes its own `relayout-runs/<stamp>/` folder, prints `error: relayout failed while <step>: <message>; every moved copy, the manifest and the links are back as they were` and exits 2. If that restore fails too, the line names the path it could not restore, keeps the backup, names `relayout-runs/<stamp>` as the place that holds every moved copy and the manifest as it was, and the run exits 2. If everything is back but the backup folder cannot be removed, the line says so, names the backup and the reason, and the run exits 2.\n136:`apply`, a capture, an adopt, a relayout, a live re-pin and a rollback each take `<overlayDir>/.build-lock` and remove it when they finish, also on failure. The owner line records the process id and the host that wrote it, as `pid=<n> host=<name>`. A lock whose owner has ended on this host is leftover state, so the next run takes it over and prints one note naming whose lock it was. A `repin --stage` does not take it: it reads a dead owner's line, prints `note: the overlay lock belongs to a run that has ended:`, and leaves the lock for the next writing run. A line with a process id and no `host=` token came from an engine that only ever ran on the machine it was installed in, so that id is probed here too. The takeover runs behind `<overlayDir>/.build-lock.takeover`, and only one run at a time can create that file. A marker older than a minute is what a run killed mid-takeover leaves behind, so the next run clears it instead of waiting for it to go. Neither leftover is deleted by its shared path. The lock and the marker are each renamed aside first and deleted only when the renamed file is the one that was judged stale, and a file another run wrote in the meantime is put back, unless another file already stands at the path, in which case the set-aside file is dropped. A marker put back this way can outlive its run, and a takeover then refuses for up to a minute and says to run it again. A run killed in that instant can leave a `.claim` file beside the lock, which is safe to delete. A lock with a live owner, a lock another host wrote, or a lock with no process id in its line still refuses, and that refusal prints the owner line and the lock file path. Remove such a file by hand once you know its run has ended.\n188:`/doctor:overlay apply --adopt <path>` turns the path into a manifest entry. It copies the file or folder into the overlay folder at its layout path, under `framework/` for a path under `.skilled/` and under `root/` for any other, with its leading dots dropped, then appends a `file` or `directory` entry and applies. An upstream file is pinned to upstream's bytes at the manifest's pin, read from the checkout `pin.checkout` names, so a later re-pin reports it as `upstream-moved`. A folder's token-named source files go into the entry's `protectedPaths`. Adopt refuses a generated path, a path an overlay already owns, a `.env` file, a token-named file that is not source or prose, a folder that holds upstream files, and a path whose copy would land under `root/skilled/`.\n263:The layout path puts each copy under `framework/` when its destination sits under `.skilled/` and under `root/` otherwise. A tree whose overlay folder still keeps its copies at the older paths, the destination with its leading dots dropped and no prefix, needs no change to keep working:\n267:- The one action is `/doctor:overlay apply --relayout`, run once. It moves every copy `offLayout` lists, backs each one up first, and points the manifest at the new paths. The next `status` prints no `LAYOUT` line.\n268:- Skipping it breaks nothing. Until it runs, though, `apply --adopt` writes each new entry at its `framework/` or `root/` path, so the folder holds both layouts side by side until the relayout moves the older copies.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015dUKfGu2ZWG6i9eYDT7Heu","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
